# PR#45: the claim is true and the remedy is worse than what it fixes. Cancelling a
# context does not un-send bytes Redis has already buffered, so an XADD can be appended
# after the moment it carries has expired. Rejecting it server-side means expressing the
# expiry in Redis's clock, and the connector is not on that machine: it would have to
# read TIME first and carry an offset, which is another round trip and a race of its own,
# to put a scripted append on the path of every event for the sake of one class of them.
#
# And it would still not be enough, which is the part that decides this. The delay is not
# only Redis's event loop. A consumer that is behind renders the same stale indicator
# from a perfectly prompt append, and no rule on the write side bounds the time between
# an event happening and somebody seeing it. Server-side rejection would close one of the
# ways to be late and leave the others.
